  "response": "When a victim organization and the security vendor it has retained disagree about the initial access vector, responders treat the disagreement as a question of recoverable evidence rather than of institutional preference. The victim organization attributed the intrusion to an employee credential stolen in a phishing campaign, and the security vendor attributed the intrusion to an internet-facing Citrix remote access appliance missing a security update; a stolen-credential origin cannot be squared with an unpatched-appliance origin, so the two attributions cannot both be true. Cloud identity platforms retain detailed sign-in logs indefinitely by default. Because that indefinite retention guarantees that the relevant record still exists, and because the organization's own account points to a credential stolen by phishing, incident responders collect authentication logs from the organization's identity provider to look for anomalous logins.\n\nThe vendor's competing account, placing the origin in an unpatched internet-facing appliance, is what drives the host-side collection that follows. Hashes of files on the appliance were compared against the vendor's known-good software manifest, a comparison set in motion by the acquisition of forensic disk and memory images from the external-facing appliance before it is rebuilt.\n\nDiscrimination between the two vectors then rests on characteristic artifact patterns. A credential-based intrusion typically shows a successful authentication from an unusual geolocation with no accompanying exploit traffic. Host-side artifacts are catalogued in the same review. Exploitation of an unpatched server typically leaves web shell files or anomalous child processes spawned by the web service account. The manifest comparison carried out on the appliance is what evidences that exploitation pattern in practice: files resident on the device but absent from the vendor's known-good list are precisely the web shell artifacts such an attack leaves behind.\n\nEndpoint detection and response agents capture full packet payloads for all network traffic on a host. Netflow records identify the first inbound connection to the appliance's management interface. Log management practice bears on investigations of this kind, and short log retention periods can destroy evidence of the initial access event before responders arrive. Because that full payload capture is available on every monitored host, responders build a chronological timeline correlating the earliest malicious activity with the earliest unauthorized access event; the timeline is assembled after netflow has pinpointed the first inbound connection to the management interface, and it remains achievable even though short retention windows can erase the record of initial access before investigators are on site.\n\nThat timeline is what disposes of the competing attributions. Either the appliance was patched before the intrusion began, or the appliance remained unpatched at the time of the intrusion; one of these must hold and both cannot, and the ordering of the first unauthorized access against the first exploit attempt determines which one the evidence supports.",
